<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">West Indies</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The West Indies are the islands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> Sea, from the Bahamas and the Greater Antilles to the Lesser Antilles, named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a>'s belief that he had reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. From the 16th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> colonies there worked by enslaved Africans and trading with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. The British islands formed the short-lived West Indies Federation from 1958 to 1962.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/west-indies.jpg" alt="West Indies" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heraldry · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:West_Indies_(orthographic_projection).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Danish trading companies dealt in Asian tea, textiles and sugar from the <b>West Indies</b>, much of it re-exported.</blockquote><a href="/empire/denmark-norway">Denmark-Norway · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Saint-Domingue became the richest colony of the French <b>West Indies</b>, the Pearl of the Antilles.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The people now called the Taíno lived across most of the <b>West Indies</b> when Europeans arrived, from the Lucayan Archipelago through Cuba, Jamaica, Hispaniola and Puerto Rico to Guadeloupe.</blockquote><a href="/culture/taino">Taíno · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In London, the imperial capital, students, lawyers and clergy from West Africa and the <b>West Indies</b> met one another and visiting Black Americans and came to see their struggles as one.</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Federations created by colonial powers, such as the <b>West Indies</b> Federation and the Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland, failed within a few years, and in the 19th century Gran Colombia and the Federal Republic of Central America had dissolved.</blockquote><a href="/government/federation">Federation · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>By 1778 French ships carried some 13,000 captives a year to the French <b>West Indies</b>, and Saint-Domingue alone held 406,000–465,000 enslaved people around 1789; there deaths outran births, as five to ten in every hundred died each year.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancien-regime-france">Social Hierarchy of Ancien Régime France</a></li></ul></section>
